%I A400276 #21 Sep 29 2026 23:51:24
%S A400276 1,4,8,9,12,16,18,20,24,25,27,28,32,36,40,44,45,48,49,50,52,54,56,63,
%T A400276 64,68,72,75,76,80,81,88,92,96,98,99,100,104,108,112,116,117,120,121,
%U A400276 124,125,128,135,136,144,147,148,152,153,160,162,164,168,169,171
%N A400276 Numbers k such that A046660(k) >= A056169(k).
%H A400276 Santi García Cremades, <a href="/A400276/b400276.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>.
%H A400276 Santi García Cremades, <a href="/A400276/a400276.pdf">Números cuñaos: de las repeticiones a una ley de distribución</a>, unpublished manuscript, 2026 (in Spanish).
%F A400276 For k >= 1 with prime factorization k = Product_{i=1..r} p_i^e_i, membership is equivalent to Sum_{i=1..r} (e_i - 1) >= #{i : e_i = 1}, or equivalently Sum_{i : e_i >= 2} e_i >= r. For k = 1, r = 0 and the empty sums are 0.
%F A400276 Equivalently, k >= 1 is a term if and only if A275812(k) >= A001221(k).
%F A400276 Let N(x) = #{n >= 1 : a(n) <= x}. Then N(x) ~ c*x/sqrt(log(x)), where c = (2*Pi)^(-1/2) * Product_{p prime > 2} ((1 - 1/p)^(1/2) * (1 + 1/(2*p) + 2/(p*(p - 2)))) = 0.7278... . The product converges absolutely and log denotes the natural logarithm.
%F A400276 For x >= 1, N(x) = C(x) + 1, where C(x) is the counting function in the linked manuscript, which excludes 1. Thus the asymptotic formula follows from Theorem 1 of that manuscript.
%e A400276 1 is a term because A046660(1) = A056169(1) = 0.
%e A400276 4 = 2^2 is a term: there is one additional copy of a prime factor and no prime factor occurring exactly once.
%e A400276 12 = 2^2*3 is a term: one additional copy balances one prime factor occurring exactly once.
%e A400276 60 = 2^2*3*5 and 90 = 2*3^2*5 are not terms: each has one additional copy but two prime factors occurring exactly once.
%e A400276 120 = 2^3*3*5 is a term: two additional copies balance two prime factors occurring exactly once.
%t A400276 A400276Q[k_] := k == 1 || Plus @@ # - Length[#] >= Count[#, 1] & [FactorInteger[k][[All, 2]]];
%t A400276 Select[Range[200], A400276Q] (* _Paolo Xausa_, Sep 29 2026 *)
%o A400276 (Python)
%o A400276 from sympy import factorint
%o A400276 def ok(k):
%o A400276     if k < 1:
%o A400276         return False
%o A400276     exponents = list(factorint(k).values())
%o A400276     return sum(e - 1 for e in exponents) >= exponents.count(1)
%o A400276 print([k for k in range(1, 172) if ok(k)])
%o A400276 (PARI) isok(k) = my(f=factor(k)); bigomega(f)-omega(f) >= #select(x->x==1, f[,2]); \\ _Michel Marcus_, Sep 29 2026
%Y A400276 Subsequence of A162966. Contains every term of A001694.
